Title: Qualitative and Quantitative Economic Development in Developing Countries: The Impact of Political Turnover Uncertainty
Abstract: This paper empirically examines the impact of political turnover uncertainty on economic development (both quantitative and qualitative) in 144 developing countries from 1996 to 2020. Utilizing a fixed-effects model and two-stage least squares, we account for cross-country heterogeneity and endogeneity issues. Our findings reveal that political turnover uncertainty delays economic development by 10% in quantitative terms and 11% in qualitative terms. This delay intensifies in countries with fewer than three presidents between 1990 and 2020. In such cases, uncertainty reduces quantitative economic development by 45% and qualitative development by 16%. Conversely, in countries with more than three presidents, the impact on economic development is weak insignificant for quantitative development and significant for qualitative development. These findings are particularly relevant for policymakers and voters in developing countries, who must address political turnover uncertainty to foster both quantitative and qualitative economic progress.
